ics and health</article-title><source>Health Policy in Australia</source><year>2002</year><publisher-name>South Melbourne: Oxford University Press</publisher-name><fpage>49</fpage><lpage>78</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Swerissen</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Duckett</surname><given-names>SJ</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Gardner H, Barraclough S</surname></name></person-group><article-title>Health policy and financing</article-title><source>Health Policy in Australia</source><year>2002</year><publisher-name>Melbourne: Oxford University Press</publisher-name><fpage>13</fpage><lpage>48</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hancock</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Hancock L</surname></name></person-group><article-title>Policy, power and interests</article-title><source>Health Policy in the Market State</source><year>1999</year><publisher-name>St Leonards, NSW: Allen &#x00026; Unwin</publisher-name><fpage>19</fpage><lpage>47</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lewis</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name></person-group><source>Health Policy and Politics: Networks, Ideas and Power</source><year>2005</year><publisher-name>East Hawthorn: IP Communications</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ham</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Ham C</surname></name></person-group><article-title>The background</article-title><source>Health Care Reform: Learning from International Experience</source><year>1997</year><publisher-name>Bristol, PA: Open University Press</publisher-name><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>20</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ross</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Snasdell-Taylor</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Cass</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Azmi</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><source>Health Financing in Australia: The Objectives and the Players</source><year>1999</year><publisher-name>Canberra: Commonwealth Department of Health and Aged Care</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hall</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Incremental change in the Australian Health Care System</article-title><source>Health Aff</source><year>1999</year><volume>18</volume><fpage>95</fpage><lpage>109</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1377/hlthaff.18.3.95</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><collab>Australian Public Service Commission</collab></person-group><source>The Australian Experience of Public Sector Reform Occasional Paper Two</source><year>2003</year><publisher-name>Commonwealth of Australia</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><collab>Australian Public Service Commission</collab></person-group><source>Connecting Government: Whole of Government Responses to Australia's Priority Challenges</source><year>2004</year><publisher-name>Management Advisory Committee, APSC, Commonwealth of Australia</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><citation citation-type="other"><person-group person-group-type="author"><collab>State Services Commission</c